    {
      "id": "be-s6-att-pricing-examples-q-a11a9d9e",
      "type": "long",
      "stem": "Using the long distance example, explain how elasticities help a manager with pricing and with reacting to competitors.",
      "options": [],
      "answer": "",
      "accept": [],
      "explanation": "",
      "model_answer_md": "**Introduction.** Elasticities turn pricing questions into numbers.\n\n**Own price elasticity.** With -8.64, demand is elastic, so lowering price raises revenue. A 3% cut raises call volume by 25.92%.\n\n**Cross price elasticity.** With 9.06, a 4% price cut by competitors reduces AT&T demand by 36.24%, because the services are substitutes.\n\n**Other uses.** Pricing, managing cash flows, competitors' prices, booms and recessions, and advertising.\n\n**Conclusion.** A manager who knows these elasticities can forecast both own-price and rival-price effects.",
      "rubric": [
        {
          "point": "Elastic, so lower price",
          "source_ref": "be--session-6#33"
        },
        {
          "point": "25.92% rise in calls",
          "source_ref": "be--session-6#35"
        },
        {
          "point": "36.24% fall from competitors' cut",
          "source_ref": "be--session-6#37"
        },
